      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2013 by the American Society for Dermatologic Surgery, Inc. Published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The treatment of ingrown toenail is usually bothersome for patients and doctors."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "To compare two treatment techniques of ingrown toenails-phenol matricectomy (PM) and nail-splinting using a flexible tube (FT)-in terms of efficacy, postoperative pain, postoperative cosmetic satisfaction, amount of tissue damage, and recurrence."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS AND MATERIALS",
            "content": "One hundred twenty ingrown toenails were randomized and divided into PM and FT groups. All cases were evaluated 2 days and 1 and 6 months after treatment. Postoperative pain, cosmetic satisfaction, time to recovery, and recurrence rate were measured."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Postoperative pain was less and cosmetic satisfaction was good in both groups (p <.001). Our recurrence rate was 8.4%. There were no statistical differences between groups in these measures. Tissue improvement rate was 6% in the PM group and 93% in the FT group 2 days after the procedures (p <.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Although FT provides faster recovery and less postoperative morbidity than PM, the techniques are equally effective in treating ingrown toenails."
          }
        ]
      },
